have a different direction on this idea,

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:41.230
how do you sort of present and get the
other three people to come along with it.

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:44.160
So, I think that was definitely
a highlight from me.

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:45.960
&gt;&gt; What was the product
just out of curiosity,

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:48.670
for those who don't know
how start apprentice?

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:51.600
&gt;&gt; So, so, we started with an idea.

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:55.830
So, our idea was basically as I mentioned,
we worked last summer.

00:15:57.190 --> 00:16:03.720
I also have an art major, so Our idea was
basically there's this crazy stat that

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:09.390
is only 5% of films that are produced
actually make it to theaters.

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:12.630
Now, obviously different,
nothing's in theaters right now, but so

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:17.480
there's basically all this content that's
out there that basically doesn't find

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:21.490
distribution and might go direct to
Netflix or direct to another platform.

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:23.890
And so, we were basically interested in

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:27.950
is there a way to basically
leverage underutilized spaces.

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:35.070
So, say like a coffee shop that closes
down at 7PM, could we rent it until nine?

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:39.007
Bring a portable projector, and
basically stream a movie for

00:16:39.007 --> 00:16:40.680
a budding film director.

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:44.742
And so, we're kind of interested in this
kind of Airbnb for small format cinema.

00:16:44.742 --> 00:16:46.355
&gt;&gt; That's cool.

00:16:46.355 --> 00:16:50.531
&gt;&gt; We called ourself Minima, actually
the company is sort of pivoted and

00:16:50.531 --> 00:16:53.662
I'm no longer involved which
I can expand on later.

00:16:53.662 --> 00:16:58.478
But yeah, it was a really just
fascinating experience with kind of

00:16:58.478 --> 00:17:04.350
the intersection of business in
which is where I'm interested in.

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:05.510
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:06.370
How about you Jack?

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:08.410
How did your leadership
transform at the GSB?

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:11.650
&gt;&gt; Yeah, great question.

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:13.480
I think, for me,

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:18.380
the course that was most impactful was
touchy feely or interpersonal dynamics.

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:19.350
I think the reason for

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:24.350
that is because the GSB teaches you
many leadership models but doesn't tell

00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:29.290
you there is a right way because it's
all individual to you as a leader.

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:32.110
So I think, leading to that then,
interpersonal dynamics for

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:36.700
me was the most impactful because
it helped me understand myself and

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:41.820
the impact that I have on people out
of all the courses at the GSB the most.

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:44.560
Which I can then transition to,
well, how do I then show up?

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:47.790
And what does that mean in
terms of my leadership style?

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:51.310
This was a very interesting
class as a Brit,

00:17:51.310 --> 00:17:53.775
the thought of been sat
in a room with 11 people.

00:17:53.775 --> 00:17:55.992
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But no real agenda, actually.

00:17:55.992 --> 00:17:57.810
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So what we think about each other,

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:04.690
at least Gary, is we have a way in the UK
of saying everything but what we think.

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:09.270
So for me, it's just fascinating to really
spend time with a group of peers and

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:12.730
learn about the impact that you have on
them and how other people impact you.

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:15.480
And take those learnings and
then apply it to well,

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:19.440
how does that mean that I show
up as a person and as a leader?

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:20.730
&gt;&gt; Is there an example?

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:21.760
I'm not going to put you on the spot.

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:24.040
You don't have to give us,

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:26.970
sometimes it's hard to understand
what the concrete learning is.

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:32.020
And I think that wasn't a question of
something you learned and touchy feely.

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:36.880
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think one of
the things I learned was that,

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:41.330
I'll give you a really basic example.

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:43.800
I use humor a lot to defuse situations.

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:48.120
And sometimes that works and
sometimes it doesn't.

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:53.090
And so, what was really great for
me was understanding when

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:58.710
taking the heat out of a situation or
using humor to build the group is useful.

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:00.980
And actually,
when it's [INAUDIBLE] tractor.

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:05.380
And so, that was just one example of
hundreds of things that I learned, but

00:19:05.380 --> 00:19:10.160
just getting enough reps to understand,
okay, this is a really useful skill.

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:12.890
But I have to choose when to
use it at the right time.

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:13.560
&gt;&gt; I love that.

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:17.190
So, it sounds like managing conflict
productively, and what are the tools,

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:18.590
that's great.

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:19.130
Thank you.

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:22.170
I'm going to go to the next question.

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:28.950
So, Diego's asking from New Jersey,
can you spend a little time

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:32.040
telling us about why you decided
to go to business school, so

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:35.900
some have pivoted in industries and
others haven't.

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:38.350
And wanted a little insight into that.

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:42.750
And Will, maybe we'll start with you,
because you didn't pivot industries and

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:44.070
decide to go to business school.

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:45.200
Maybe you can share that.

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:50.320
Yeah, I mean, for me,
I think there were kind of three things

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:52.910
that led me to take
the business school experience.

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:58.580
One was I wanted the kind of safe
opportunity to test out other things.

00:19:58.580 --> 00:20:01.800
Even though I knew I was going back
to Bain, business school was kind of

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:05.380
an awesome way for me to think about
what I wanted to do long term.

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:08.150
So, I did my Master's in
education at the same time.

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:11.720
And I got the chance to work with a lot
of different nonprofits while I was in

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:16.450
the school and kind of understand where I
want to fit into that space down the road.

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:17.490
And then the second thing for

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:23.080
me was to build a network outside of
just my college peers and my company.

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:26.820
And in particular, build a network that
was going to be international, and

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:28.270
that has been really amazing.

00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:32.140
We can talk a little bit about it later,
but I think that the GSP network is

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:35.850
more powerful than I could ever
even have possibly imagined.

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:38.980
I remember like two weeks into school,
someone posted on our

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:42.670
Slack channel that one of her friends
had gotten into trouble in South Sudan.

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:44.800
And she was like,
do you know anyone we can call?

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:48.480
And within like five minutes, like three
or four people had given her the names of

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:52.190
like diplomats or ambassadors or
people in South Sudan.

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:56.210
And I was like, well, if the GSP can
give us contacts in South Sudan, there's

00:20:56.210 --> 00:21:00.980
probably nowhere in the world we're not
going to have kind of people at our back.

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:01.600
And then for

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:05.800
me finally, I was just really kind of
burnt out after three years of work.

00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:09.070
I was tired,
I wanted the chance to kind of refresh.

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:13.210
And what I found was that Stanford got me
excited again, about a lot of the things

00:21:13.210 --> 00:21:16.450
that I was working on, helped me to
step back, think about my career.

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:19.480
Is not like a of couple years
is a hard work, but really,

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:23.460
it's kind of a 40 year journey and all
the interesting places that could take me.

00:21:23.460 --> 00:21:25.364
So, those were the reasons
that I ended up, and

00:21:25.364 --> 00:21:29.060
I think the GSB kind of knocked it out
of the park in all of those dimensions.

00:21:29.060 --> 00:21:31.760
&gt;&gt; Awesome, and
how does it feel to be back at Bain now?

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:33.850
How are you different in the role?

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:35.719
So, that's why you went.

00:21:35.719 --> 00:21:37.666
If you look back-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:21:37.666 --> 00:21:40.077
&gt;&gt; What was the value for you?

00:21:40.077 --> 00:21:43.380
&gt;&gt; One day, I think that I was
talking to a few people about this,

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:47.190
who had done consulting and then got back
to very similar roles in consulting, and

00:21:47.190 --> 00:21:49.330
we all felt way better at our jobs.

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:52.290
And that actually, in some ways,
was not something that we were expecting.

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:55.535
There were a lot of things we
are expecting out of this school,

00:21:55.535 --> 00:21:58.550
I actually wasn't expecting to
feel that much better because so

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:00.760
much of what you learn
is learned on the job.

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:05.710
But I think it really helped with
maturity, with being able to step back.

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:09.310
And during the school you
have the chance to examine so

00:22:09.310 --> 00:22:12.340
many cases, to meet with so
many important speakers

00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:15.470
that it almost feels like it
gave me like greater gravitas.

00:22:15.470 --> 00:22:19.210
And a greater ability to bring in
experiences that I had seen at the GSB.

00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:22.580
And I've actually used a lot of what
I learned in classes, frameworks or

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:23.360
ways of thinking or

00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:28.320
case studies and that has surprised me,
been kind of a nice and pleasant surprise.

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:29.490
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:30.330
How about Michelle?

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:32.930
I didn't give you a heads up early.

00:22:32.930 --> 00:22:38.850
How are you thinking about pivoting
from into gaming and product marketing?

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:39.410
&gt;&gt; Yeah, totally.

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:44.400
So, I mentioned I was at Accenture for
three years.

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:47.200
But my background is somewhat unique.

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:49.766
I was told, at least at my university,

00:22:49.766 --> 00:22:53.930
I was the only one that has ever
majored in finance and fine arts.

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:55.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, my majors,

00:22:55.760 --> 00:23:00.160
just the story behind that is I'm
passionate about both things.

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:03.490
I love business, I think like
business is kind of everything.

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:07.398
But then, I also love, I'm a film junkie,
I love going to Sundance,

00:23:07.398 --> 00:23:10.590
I actually lived in Park City for
four years.

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:14.070
And so, I knew I always had
this interest in entertainment.

00:23:14.070 --> 00:23:18.880
I did work with a few media entertainment
clients when I was at Accenture,

00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:23.610
and I really liked that, it was like
the most hip clients I kind of had.

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:28.520
But I knew I wanted to do something
smaller and something more creative.

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:32.280
I can't say I felt super creative and

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:35.460
my role was really leveraging,
kind of playing to that strength.

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:38.900
And so, I came back to business school.

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:44.210
I was interested in one kind of
pivoting industries, but also being

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:48.940
more in an operating role, in a product
role approach to business and operations.

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:53.110
And so, I ended up sort of, for
the summer Trying something out

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:57.880
at WeWork in an operating role and
actually really liked that type of role.

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:00.240
But I wasn't quite in
the industry that I wanted and

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:04.320
so was able to find something at
a small gaming startup for full time.

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:09.130
And so, I think it was a result of
lots of networking with alumni,

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:11.059
just speaking with them.

00:24:11.059 --> 00:24:15.682
I mentioned the startup garage
experience really impacting kind of my

00:24:15.682 --> 00:24:17.760
interest in entertainment.

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:24.070
And specifically gaming and so that was
sort of my rational part coming back and

00:24:24.070 --> 00:24:26.950
hopefully it goes well when I
start in a couple of weeks.

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:27.840
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:32.420
And the next question was
about the culture and

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:35.760
Aditi I'm going to to ask this one to you.

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:39.510
Alyssa is asking what makes
a GSB culture unique and

00:24:39.510 --> 00:24:42.790
what sets it apart from
other MBA programs?

00:24:42.790 --> 00:24:48.780
So I don't expect you to speak about other
programs that I as a dean of admissions,

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:50.140
I keep calling it magic.

00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:54.190
And that's pretty high
level how you describe it,

00:24:54.190 --> 00:24:58.470
so what tactically is it like
to be part of the culture?

00:24:58.470 --> 00:24:59.240
&gt;&gt; My gosh.

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:02.350
I mean, it is magical,

00:25:02.350 --> 00:25:08.415
it's hard to sometimes
sum it up into words but

00:25:08.415 --> 00:25:13.235
I think what really stands out to me is

00:25:13.235 --> 00:25:17.759
how supportive of a program it is.

00:25:17.759 --> 00:25:21.840
I think I was always
interested in getting my MBA.

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:25.590
I, over the course of like the first
few years that I was working,

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:30.650
got a better understanding of what
I hoped to get out of the program.

00:25:30.650 --> 00:25:36.060
Many, many reasons for being kind of
similar to Will in terms of, really,

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:38.130
it's a safe space to experiment and learn.

00:25:38.130 --> 00:25:42.760
I used to look at the other people
who were [INAUDIBLE] who had MBAs and

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:46.974
really admired their skills in
terms of how to manage teams or

00:25:46.974 --> 00:25:51.707
work with teams, their presence,
their ability to present well.

00:25:51.707 --> 00:25:56.431
But I think what I just didn't
expect going into it was how

00:25:56.431 --> 00:26:00.960
I'd come out of it with
the closest friends of my life,

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:04.420
truly, truly like my lifelong friends.

00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:11.570
I got married a couple years ago, and
most of my bridal party was from the GSB.

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:15.140
And I didn't expect that
going into business school.

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:18.673
I kind of thought I'd found my closest
friends in college and everything but

00:26:18.673 --> 00:26:20.780
I think there's just something different.

00:26:20.780 --> 00:26:28.820
And maybe part of it's in some ways a very
intense two years right, it's busy.

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:35.120
Between classes and extracurriculars and
the social component, it's quite busy.

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:39.841
And then the kinds of classes you're
taking, to go through experiences like

00:26:39.841 --> 00:26:44.563
interpersonal dynamics, they're not
easy courses or startup garage where

00:26:44.563 --> 00:26:49.374
you're really learning how to kind of
work with each other in a different way.

00:26:49.374 --> 00:26:55.750
I think what I just didn't expect was how
helpful and supportive everyone would be.

00:26:55.750 --> 00:26:59.210
You ask somebody for
guidance or for advice or

00:26:59.210 --> 00:27:03.450
you want to talk to them about what it was
like to work in this industry or that or

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:06.400
you don't know how to do
your operations homework or

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:10.880
your accounting homework and
everyone will drop everything to help you.

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:13.870
And I think that's pretty special.

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:19.090
And I can't speak to whether that sets it
apart necessarily from other programs, but

00:27:19.090 --> 00:27:24.290
it I guess it went beyond my expectations.

00:27:25.830 --> 00:27:28.330
&gt;&gt; Is there someone else
who wants to jump in?

00:27:28.330 --> 00:27:30.920
Any of the other panelists who
feels strongly about the culture?

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:33.370
All right, Jack.

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:36.752
&gt;&gt; I can quickly jump in.

00:27:36.752 --> 00:27:41.614
So the community feel is amazing,
as was just described, but

00:27:41.614 --> 00:27:47.222
one small example would be I'd spoken
to a friend and said I'm thinking

00:27:47.222 --> 00:27:52.570
of recruiting for these kind of
roles in passing in the coffee shop.

00:27:52.570 --> 00:27:57.319
And then two days later I'm walking
through campus through town square and

00:27:57.319 --> 00:27:59.685
he stops me and he says, well just so

00:27:59.685 --> 00:28:03.705
you know, these two companies
are on campus in the next two days.

00:28:03.705 --> 00:28:07.265
I spoke to them, I gave them your name,
let me know if you need any help.

00:28:07.265 --> 00:28:11.825
Let me know if you want to chat
through anything and went on his way.

00:28:11.825 --> 00:28:15.695
I'd forgotten I even told him the type
of thing that I was interested in.

00:28:15.695 --> 00:28:18.445
And he'd gone out of his way
then to think about who he knew,

00:28:18.445 --> 00:28:22.025
think about who he could put me in touch
with, coached me through the process.

00:28:23.090 --> 00:28:24.677
And that's not unusual.

00:28:24.677 --> 00:28:28.315
For homework, for people, for
events, for trips, for any class,

00:28:28.315 --> 00:28:32.397
I think people are always thinking
about their classmates, their friends,

00:28:32.397 --> 00:28:35.320
how they can help and support and
how they can be there.

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:39.320
I think that's just one example in
a professional context of how it happened,

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:41.990
but it happens in a personal
context all the time.

00:28:41.990 --> 00:28:42.560
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:28:43.780 --> 00:28:48.340
I want to make sure we cover
the global experience.

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:53.180
So Eric, maybe you could, you're nodding,
I see your head, do you want to share?

00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:54.710
We have a global experience requirement.

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:58.420
There are many ways that you
can fulfill that as a student,

00:28:58.420 --> 00:28:59.970
but perhaps talk to what you did?

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:03.050
&gt;&gt; Yeah, happy to.

00:29:03.050 --> 00:29:08.040
So I chose to do a couple of
experiences both formal and informal.

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:11.500
Informal as many classmates were from

00:29:12.910 --> 00:29:17.090
various countries wanted to create
experiences for people to come and

00:29:17.090 --> 00:29:22.330
walk a little bit in their shoes and those
were some of my favorite experiences.

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:24.830
Two foreign ones, I did the step
program which was an exchange

00:29:24.830 --> 00:29:27.340
program with Sang Mai University in China.

00:29:27.340 --> 00:29:32.870
I also did a global study trip,
to Mexico and

00:29:32.870 --> 00:29:39.770
the Mexico trip,
I had been to Mexico briefly before.

00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:43.368
But it was a experience that I
could not have done anywhere else.

00:29:43.368 --> 00:29:48.350
And I think a few things
that stood out about it.

00:29:48.350 --> 00:29:53.646
One was the leaders were extremely
thoughtful and extremely vulnerable in

00:29:53.646 --> 00:29:59.790
how they shared their own personal stories
and how that intersected with the country.

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:03.310
And so I could see it not only
from my lens with classmates, but

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:05.600
also from the way they
were viewing things.

00:30:05.600 --> 00:30:12.150
So for example, one of the students
had attended Harvard and

00:30:12.150 --> 00:30:16.040
I think he had had a scholarship
there earlier on in his life.

00:30:17.060 --> 00:30:20.410
And there was a number, it became actually
a national controversy at the time,

00:30:20.410 --> 00:30:24.370
where people were talking about like hey
why are we helping this person whose

00:30:24.370 --> 00:30:31.080
family had come into the US illegally
versus someone who was born in the US?

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:35.896
And so hearing him talk about that
story and then together as a group

00:30:35.896 --> 00:30:41.061
crossing the border in Tijuana gave
it a really different experience.

00:30:41.061 --> 00:30:44.360
And so
I think like the global experiences,

00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:51.000
I think helped us really to go to a much
deeper level in understanding each other.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:54.070
And then they were also extremely fun and

00:30:54.070 --> 00:30:58.090
we went to like just great places we
wouldn't have been to otherwise and

00:30:58.090 --> 00:31:00.710
the chance to spend so
much time with each other.

00:31:00.710 --> 00:31:03.170
Going through these once
in a lifetime experiences,

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:06.860
I think was one of the major ways that
I made some of my closest friends.

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:08.660
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:31:09.740 --> 00:31:13.856
I'm looking at the time and I'd like all
of you to give me a wrap up comment.

00:31:13.856 --> 00:31:19.880
And I think the one of the questions that

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:24.260
came in is if you had any words of advice,
so if someone's admitted to the GSB, and

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:30.040
you're looking back, what advice would
you give them about coming to the school?

00:31:30.040 --> 00:31:32.670
So how about I'll start with you Will.

00:31:32.670 --> 00:31:33.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure thing.

00:31:33.470 --> 00:31:35.770
Well, so first of all,
you should definitely come.

00:31:35.770 --> 00:31:37.550
I know some people get
into business school and

00:31:37.550 --> 00:31:38.820
they're like, like should I do it?

00:31:38.820 --> 00:31:39.930
It's definitely worth it.

00:31:39.930 --> 00:31:42.800
And there was not a single
person I knew in my class who

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:45.570
didn't think it was
worth it by like a mile.

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:51.095
The one other thing I'd say is, it makes
a lot of sense to Like plan in advance,

00:31:51.095 --> 00:31:54.351
because it is really like
an overwhelming experience.

00:31:54.351 --> 00:31:55.534
There's so much going on.

00:31:55.534 --> 00:31:57.679
There's so
many interesting people to meet,

00:31:57.679 --> 00:32:01.316
that I think it's really helpful to have
a sense of what you want to get out of it.

00:32:01.316 --> 00:32:05.666
And it's really okay if what you want to
get out of it is, trying something or

00:32:05.666 --> 00:32:09.548
testing something that turns out to
not be at all what you want to do.

00:32:09.548 --> 00:32:13.533
There's always you know, that's
actually a really helpful learning, but

00:32:13.533 --> 00:32:15.686
I think it's worth planning in advance.

00:32:15.686 --> 00:32:20.293
And then the other thing I think is just
like throwing yourself in and not letting

00:32:20.293 --> 00:32:24.276
it slip by, I mean when I look back at
the GSB I have very few regrets but

00:32:24.276 --> 00:32:28.262
the only things I regret are like if
anything, not trying to do more or

00:32:28.262 --> 00:32:30.993
not making more time to
do all of those things.

00:32:30.993 --> 00:32:35.070
It's really such a unique to your
experience surrounded by incredible people

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:38.414
with unlimited resources and
opportunities at your disposal.

00:32:38.414 --> 00:32:40.331
So I think you want to
make the most of that

00:32:47.332 --> 00:32:47.900
&gt;&gt; Hey,

00:32:47.900 --> 00:32:53.714
Dita How about you give us a wrap
on this question too please.

00:32:53.714 --> 00:32:57.103
Some advice.

00:32:57.103 --> 00:32:57.950
&gt;&gt; Sure yeah.

00:32:57.950 --> 00:33:01.927
So I think I would say that let's see,

00:33:01.927 --> 00:33:06.661
it's going to say kind
of what Bill was saying.

00:33:06.661 --> 00:33:11.161
But I mean, I think I'm being really
open to new experiences, I mean,

00:33:11.161 --> 00:33:14.686
I think people have different
comfort levels of how much

00:33:14.686 --> 00:33:19.486
they want to trying new things versus
not versus kind of sticking with like,

00:33:19.486 --> 00:33:23.029
the types of classes that
they know they'll do well in.

00:33:23.029 --> 00:33:26.817
Or like the types of friends that they
used to have prior to business school.

00:33:26.817 --> 00:33:31.654
I just think that it's such an opportunity
to push yourself outside of your

00:33:31.654 --> 00:33:36.642
comfort zone, take that class that you
don't have a background in at all, or

00:33:36.642 --> 00:33:41.597
spend time with people who just aren't
like the rest of your friends so far.

00:33:41.597 --> 00:33:44.593
There's never going to really be
another opportunity to do that, and

00:33:44.593 --> 00:33:47.206
it might surprise you in terms
of what you find that you like.

00:33:51.579 --> 00:33:55.489
&gt;&gt; I'm looking at the time anybody else
want to jump in Eric, Michelle or Jack?

00:33:55.489 --> 00:33:57.720
What's some advice?

00:33:57.720 --> 00:33:58.627
Okay.

00:33:58.627 --> 00:34:02.589
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's it's sort of similar but,
yeah, I completely agree

00:34:02.589 --> 00:34:07.350
with the comments about being open to
a bunch of different types of experience.

00:34:07.350 --> 00:34:12.082
But if there's one thing that
can summarize the last two

00:34:12.082 --> 00:34:14.605
years it is incredibly busy.

00:34:14.605 --> 00:34:19.973
And I think just having the ability
to focus, and have maybe a few

00:34:19.973 --> 00:34:25.853
things that you really know you want to
prioritize and get involved in.

00:34:25.853 --> 00:34:29.956
I think, just an example in the fall of,
second year,

00:34:29.956 --> 00:34:35.327
you're going to see a lot of your
classmates recruiting for consulting.

00:34:35.327 --> 00:34:39.913
And for a while, I was like, should I
recruit for another consulting firm, but

00:34:39.913 --> 00:34:43.763
I was like, no, no, I came here to do and
I want to stay true to that.

00:34:43.763 --> 00:34:48.100
And so
I think kind of being your own person and

00:34:48.100 --> 00:34:52.894
focusing, I took art
classes across the street,

00:34:52.894 --> 00:34:59.988
even though no one else was sort of
doing that, just the ability to focus.

00:34:59.988 --> 00:35:05.457
And, yeah, like I said, be your own person
I think is some good advice I give.

00:35:05.457 --> 00:35:06.691
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:35:06.691 --> 00:35:10.721
Jackie, were nodding your head, did you
want to say one last thing as well?

00:35:10.721 --> 00:35:13.745
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
just those ruthless priorities and

00:35:13.745 --> 00:35:18.870
make sure you have it have a good
system for holding yourself to account.

00:35:18.870 --> 00:35:24.797
I remember you know, week one student life
telling me about, proactively calendaring.

00:35:24.797 --> 00:35:30.603
I can remember being Saturday going,
I'm a grown up, I can manage my calendar.

00:35:30.603 --> 00:35:32.768
I used to 10 businesses around.

00:35:32.768 --> 00:35:34.052
And then two days later,

00:35:34.052 --> 00:35:36.997
I'm like, quadruple booked
four times in the same day.

00:35:36.997 --> 00:35:41.867
And I felt like a drink from a firehose,
so, have these disparities,

00:35:41.867 --> 00:35:45.765
find somebody outside of
the GSB to hold you to account.

00:35:45.765 --> 00:35:48.895
And somebody that you can come back to and
say,

00:35:48.895 --> 00:35:54.043
okay do I really need this brown bag lunch
or am I just going for the sandwich?

00:35:54.043 --> 00:35:55.470
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:58.026
Finally, Eric,
why don't you help us close?

00:35:58.026 --> 00:35:59.106
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:35:59.106 --> 00:36:05.703
So I'll see it's builds on what
a number of others have said.

00:36:05.703 --> 00:36:10.779
I said the main thing is slowly smooth and
smooth is fast.

00:36:10.779 --> 00:36:15.639
When you first come, there can be
an incredible rush to do everything,

00:36:15.639 --> 00:36:18.313
to try everything, to meet everyone.

00:36:18.313 --> 00:36:22.308
And I personally think that I
would have gone farther and

00:36:22.308 --> 00:36:24.263
gotten even more out of it.

00:36:24.263 --> 00:36:28.432
If I take the time to get to know
each person a little bit more.

00:36:28.432 --> 00:36:32.818
If I take in the time to think of it
not as a two year sprint, actually,

00:36:32.818 --> 00:36:35.617
like two years a meaningful
chunk of life and

00:36:35.617 --> 00:36:39.720
there's a lot that you can get
done there if you set yourself up.

00:36:39.720 --> 00:36:45.845
Where it's a way that you could continue,
if it were the rest of your life,

00:36:45.845 --> 00:36:52.455
and so I think if you find a way, even if
it's hard to prioritize, to slow down and

00:36:52.455 --> 00:36:58.679
spend that extra 30 minutes with
someone versus 20 short coffee chats,

00:36:58.679 --> 00:37:03.482
or you spend that extra time on
that case to go deeper on it.

00:37:03.482 --> 00:37:07.776
That you may find that you'll learn more
quickly what it is that you want to do,

00:37:07.776 --> 00:37:11.874
and that ultimately leads you to knowing
more people who will stay close to

00:37:11.874 --> 00:37:12.618
your heart.

00:37:12.618 --> 00:37:15.305
So I encourage you to go slow to go fast.

00:37:15.305 --> 00:37:16.389
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:37:16.389 --> 00:37:17.861
I think that's a wonderful place to end.

00:37:17.861 --> 00:37:21.291
I'm going to share just
a couple more slides but

00:37:21.291 --> 00:37:24.912
want to thank Jack,
Michelle, Will, DT, Eric.

00:37:24.912 --> 00:37:26.617
Thank you so much for being here.

00:37:26.617 --> 00:37:32.637
So Stanford here's a quick picture of
our courtyard we call it town square and

00:37:32.637 --> 00:37:34.220
the GSB buildings.

00:37:34.220 --> 00:37:37.476
And when this campus was
built just eight years ago,

00:37:37.476 --> 00:37:42.732
the cornerstone actually says, dedicated
to the things that haven't happened yet

00:37:42.732 --> 00:37:45.559
and the people who
are about to dream them up.

00:37:45.559 --> 00:37:48.569
And I think that really captures
the essence of Stanford.

00:37:48.569 --> 00:37:51.895
We hope if you've come today that you
see yourself as a big dreamer, and

00:37:51.895 --> 00:37:53.842
you'll be interested in learning more.

00:37:53.842 --> 00:37:58.396
Stanford is really built for
people like you.

00:37:58.396 --> 00:37:59.908
And if you want to learn more,

00:37:59.908 --> 00:38:02.995
you got a chance to hear from
some of our wonderful alumni.

00:38:02.995 --> 00:38:06.662
But as I said earlier, feel free to book
an information session if you really

00:38:06.662 --> 00:38:09.662
want to think about admissions,
and what we're looking for.

00:38:09.662 --> 00:38:11.242
There's also industry panels.

00:38:11.242 --> 00:38:13.679
For instance, if you are looking for

00:38:13.679 --> 00:38:19.109
a career in finance come hear from a whole
panel of finance, alumni and students.

00:38:19.109 --> 00:38:23.998
And then we have added in the last 18
months, some small group chats which is

00:38:23.998 --> 00:38:29.131
really about 10 prospects with the either
an admissions officer or students.

00:38:29.131 --> 00:38:32.990
So it's a more intimate environment where
you can get your individual questions

00:38:32.990 --> 00:38:33.567
answered.

00:38:33.567 --> 00:38:37.352
Please, if I didn't get there many
questions in the chat if I didn't get to

00:38:37.352 --> 00:38:39.407
yours, you can also email us on the web.

00:38:39.407 --> 00:38:40.872
We appreciate that.

00:38:40.872 --> 00:38:45.919
And just in closing, if you do come
to Stanford, we believe that it

00:38:45.919 --> 00:38:52.156
will really open your horizons, your self
narrative of what you can accomplish.

00:38:52.156 --> 00:38:56.488
We think will teach you to think boldly,
think innovatively, it'll help

00:38:56.488 --> 00:39:00.971
you develop your leadership capabilities
so others will want to follow you.

00:39:00.971 --> 00:39:03.889
And finally it will help you
to find the path not just for

00:39:03.889 --> 00:39:06.500
these next two years but
for the next 40 years.

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:10.043
So you can see our mission and we really
try to live it at Stanford each and

00:39:10.043 --> 00:39:10.644
every day.

00:39:10.644 --> 00:39:14.499
So thank you so much for
joining us and have a good one.

00:39:14.499 --> 00:39:20.169
[MUSIC]

